+To resample a loaded GLB *into* Gaussian splats (the other direction — this
+one does take a mesh, like `--voxel`/`--sdf`):
+
+```
+./build/italy-rs path/to/asset.glb --splat-fit=400000
+```
+
+`400000` is the total splat count (clamped to 1k..4M; the flag also works
+bare, using the current default). The mesh's surface is sampled area-uniformly
+and each sample becomes a flat disk-shaped Gaussian lying in the surface,
+coloured from the base-colour texture at that point — see
+`src/convert/mesh_to_gsplat.h`. The result goes through the same renderer path
+as `--gsplat`, so everything above about flat-diffuse shading applies. It is
+not the optimization-based fit a 3DGS trainer does; it's an analytic one-pass
+resample, which is why it runs in well under a second.
+
+Fitted (or imported) splats can be written back out as a standard 3DGS `.ply`
+with the "Export .ply" button in the controls panel, or headlessly with
+`ITALY_SPLAT_EXPORT=out.ply`. Note that this repo's loader reads `rot_0..3`
+positionally as `(x,y,z,w)` while the INRIA reference implementation writes
+them `w`-first, so files round-trip within italy but a third-party 3DGS
+viewer will read the quaternions permuted.
+
